Lab 6: Managing Hosts in RHVM
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Add multiple Red Hat Virtualization Host (RHVH) instances to Red Hat Virtualization Manager (RHVM)
Organize hosts into logical datacenters and clusters for better management
Monitor host status, resource utilization, and performance metrics
Understand the hierarchical structure of RHVM infrastructure
Perform basic host maintenance operations
Troubleshoot common host connectivity issues
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of virtualization concepts
Familiarity with Linux command line operations
Knowledge of networking fundamentals (IP addressing, DNS)
Completion of previous RHVM installation and configuration labs
Understanding of datacenter and cluster concepts in virtualization
Required Knowledge Areas
Red Hat Virtualization Architecture: Understanding of RHVM components
Network Configuration: Basic TCP/IP and DNS concepts
Linux System Administration: File permissions, service management
Storage Concepts: Shared storage and storage domains
Lab Environment Setup
Al Nafi Cloud Machines: This lab uses pre-configured Linux-based cloud machines provided by Al Nafi. Simply click Start Lab to access your environment. No need to build your own virtual machines.

Environment Details
Your lab environment includes:

1 RHVM Manager node (pre-installed and configured)
3 RHVH Host nodes (clean installations ready for management)
Shared storage system (NFS or iSCSI)
Network connectivity between all components
Task 1: Adding Multiple RHVH Hosts to RHVM
Subtask 1.1: Verify RHVM Manager Status
First, ensure your RHVM Manager is running and accessible.

Access the RHVM Web Interface
# Check if RHVM engine service is running
sudo systemctl status ovirt-engine

# If not running, start the service
sudo systemctl start ovirt-engine
Open Web Browser and Navigate to RHVM
Access the RHVM web interface at: https://your-rhvm-manager-ip/ovirt-engine

Login with Administrator Credentials
Username: admin@internal
Password: your-admin-password
Subtask 1.2: Prepare RHVH Hosts for Management
Before adding hosts to RHVM, ensure they are properly configured.

Connect to First RHVH Host
# SSH to the first RHVH host
ssh root@rhvh-host1-ip

# Verify host is ready
vdsm-client Host getStats
Configure Host Network Settings
# Check network configuration
nmcli connection show

# Ensure proper DNS resolution
nslookup your-rhvm-manager-hostname

# Verify time synchronization
chrony sources -v
Repeat for Additional Hosts
Perform the same verification steps for rhvh-host2 and rhvh-host3.

Subtask 1.3: Add First Host to RHVM
Navigate to Hosts Section
In the RHVM web interface:

Click on Compute → Hosts
Click New button
Configure Host Details
Fill in the following information:

Name: rhvh-host1
Comment: Primary virtualization host
Hostname: rhvh-host1.example.com (or IP address)
SSH Port: 22
Cluster: Default (we'll create custom clusters later)
Authentication Settings
Authentication Method: Password
Root Password: Enter the root password for the RHVH host
Advanced Parameters
# Optional: Configure host-specific settings
# Power Management: Configure if using IPMI/iLO
# Kernel: Leave default unless specific requirements
# Memory: Set memory overcommit ratio if needed
Complete Host Addition
Click OK to add the host
Monitor the installation progress in the Events tab
Subtask 1.4: Add Additional Hosts
Repeat the host addition process for the remaining hosts:

Add Second Host
Name: rhvh-host2
Hostname: rhvh-host2.example.com
Follow the same configuration steps
Add Third Host
Name: rhvh-host3
Hostname: rhvh-host3.example.com
Follow the same configuration steps
Verify Host Status
# Check all hosts are in "Up" status
# Navigate to Compute → Hosts
# Verify each host shows:
# - Status: Up
# - SPM: One host should be SPM (Storage Pool Manager)
# - Load: Should show current resource utilization
Task 2: Organizing Hosts into Datacenters and Clusters
Subtask 2.1: Understanding RHVM Hierarchy
The RHVM organizational structure follows this hierarchy:

Datacenter → Cluster → Host → Virtual Machine
Subtask 2.2: Create Additional Datacenters
Create Production Datacenter
Navigate to Compute → Data Centers:

Click New
Name: Production-DC
Description: Production environment datacenter
Type: Shared
Compatibility Version: 4.4 (or latest available)
Quota Mode: Disabled
Create Development Datacenter
Name: Development-DC
Description: Development and testing environment
Type: Shared
Compatibility Version: 4.4
Subtask 2.3: Create Clusters within Datacenters
Create Production Cluster
Navigate to Compute → Clusters:

# Production Cluster Configuration
Name: Production-Cluster
Data Center: Production-DC
Management Network: ovirtmgmt
CPU Type: Intel Nehalem Family (or appropriate for your hardware)
Compatibility Version: 4.4
Create Development Cluster
# Development Cluster Configuration
Name: Development-Cluster
Data Center: Development-DC
Management Network: ovirtmgmt
CPU Type: Intel Nehalem Family
Compatibility Version: 4.4
Configure Cluster Policies
For each cluster, configure:

Scheduling Policy: evenly_distributed
Memory Optimization: None (for production) or For Server Load (for development)
CPU Threads: Enable if supported by hardware
Subtask 2.4: Move Hosts to Appropriate Clusters
Move Host to Production Cluster
Navigate to Compute → Hosts
Select rhvh-host1
Click Edit
Change Cluster to Production-Cluster
Click OK
Distribute Remaining Hosts
# Host Distribution Strategy:
# rhvh-host1 → Production-Cluster
# rhvh-host2 → Production-Cluster  
# rhvh-host3 → Development-Cluster
Verify Host Placement
Check that hosts are properly distributed and show Up status in their new clusters.

Subtask 2.5: Configure Storage Domains for Datacenters
Attach Storage to Production Datacenter
Navigate to Storage → Domains:

Select existing storage domain
Click Manage Domain
Attach to Production-DC
Create Development Storage Domain
# If separate storage is available for development
Name: Development-Storage
Domain Function: Data
Storage Type: NFS (or available type)
Export Path: /path/to/development/storage
