Subtask 3.5: Host Maintenance Operations
Put Host in Maintenance Mode
# Before performing maintenance:
# 1. Navigate to Compute → Hosts
# 2. Select host
# 3. Click "Maintenance"
# 4. Confirm migration of running VMs
Activate Host from Maintenance
# After maintenance completion:
# 1. Select host in maintenance
# 2. Click "Activate"  
# 3. Wait for host to return to "Up" status
Host Health Checks
# Perform regular health checks
# SSH to host and run:
vdsm-client Host getStats
systemctl status vdsmd
df -h  # Check disk space
free -h  # Check memory
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Host Connection Problems
# If host fails to connect:
# 1. Check network connectivity
ping rhvh-host-ip

# 2. Verify SSH access
ssh root@rhvh-host-ip

# 3. Check firewall settings
firewall-cmd --list-all

# 4. Verify VDSM service
systemctl status vdsmd
Host Status Issues
# If host shows "Non Responsive":
# 1. Check host system load
uptime

# 2. Restart VDSM service
systemctl restart vdsmd

# 3. Check storage connectivity
mount | grep storage-domain
Performance Issues
# For performance problems:
# 1. Check system resources
top
iostat -x 1

# 2. Review logs
tail -f /var/log/vdsm/vdsm.log

# 3. Verify network performance
iperf3 -c rhvm-manager-ip
Verification Steps
Verify Host Management
Check Host Status

All hosts should show Up status
Hosts should be distributed across clusters as planned
One host per cluster should be designated as SPM
Verify Cluster Configuration

Clusters should be associated with correct datacenters
Cluster policies should be properly configured
CPU compatibility should be consistent within clusters
Test Host Operations

Successfully put host in maintenance mode
Migrate VMs between hosts
Activate host from maintenance
Validate Monitoring Setup
# Confirm monitoring is working:
# 1. Check host statistics are updating
# 2. Verify alert notifications (if configured)
# 3. Test custom monitoring scripts
# 4. Review performance trends
